drills.solution:
export function pipe(...fns) {
  return (...args) => {
    if (fns.length === 0) return args[0];
    let value = fns[0](...args);
    for (const fn of fns.slice(1)) value = fn(value);
    return value;
  };
}

tests.code:
assert.equal(typeof mod.pipe, 'function', 'export pipe');
const f = mod.pipe((s) => s.trim(), (s) => s.toLowerCase(), (s) => s.replace(/ +/g, '-'));
assert.equal(f('  Hello Big World '), 'hello-big-world');

const inc = (n) => n + 1;
const dbl = (n) => n * 2;
assert.equal(mod.pipe(inc, dbl)(5), 12);
assert.equal(mod.pipe(dbl, inc)(5), 11);

drills.explanation:
Call the first function with the spread arguments, then fold the rest over the running value:

```js
export function pipe(...fns) {
  return (...args) => {
    if (fns.length === 0) return args[0];
    let value = fns[0](...args);
    for (const fn of fns.slice(1)) value = fn(value);
    return value;
  };
}
```

`fns.reduce((v, fn) => fn(v), args[0])` is shorter but fails the test "the first function receives every argument": the first function would see only `args[0]`, so `(a, b) => a + b` gets `b` as undefined. Dropping the empty check fails "no functions returns the first argument (boundary)", because `fns[0]` is undefined and calling it throws a TypeError. Only the first function is spread over `args`; every later one gets a single value, not the argument list.

